---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { BREW_COMMAND } from "../../lib/github";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout currentSlug="quickstart" title="Quickstart" description="Install, sign in, add a repository.">
  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <p>On a Mac or Linux with Homebrew:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={BREW_COMMAND} />
  <p>
    Anything else is on the <a href={`${base}/#downloads`}>downloads list</a>. If you're not sure which file you need,
    <a href={`${base}/#pick`}>Help me pick</a> asks four questions and gives you one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sign-in">Sign in</h2>
  <p>
    The first launch looks like GitHub Desktop's: sign in to GitHub.com or GitHub Enterprise, or skip it and use local
    repositories only. You can sign in later from Settings › Accounts (<kbd>⌘,</kbd>, or File › Options on Windows and
    Linux).
  </p>
  <p>
    Signing in opens your browser. Some builds can't use the browser flow and show a one-time code to enter on GitHub
    instead. Either way the token goes into your system keychain.
  </p>

  <h2 id="repositories">Add repositories</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><kbd>⌘O</kbd> adds a repository that's already on disk.</li>
    <li><kbd>⇧⌘O</kbd> clones one, from your account, a URL or just <code>owner/name</code>.</li>
    <li><kbd>⌘N</kbd> creates a new one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On Windows and Linux it's <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> instead of <kbd>⌘</kbd>.</p>
  <p>
    Coming from GitHub Desktop? File › Import Repositories from GitHub Desktop copies its repository list, aliases
    included. Accounts don't come along, so sign in once more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cli">Command line</h2>
  <p>
    Corvene › Install Command Line Tool (File › Install Command Line Tool on Windows and Linux) gives you a <code>corvene</code> command, so
    <code>corvene .</code> opens the folder you're in. The Windows installer, .msi, .deb, .rpm and tarball set it up for
    you.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
